WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.360
Every head movement has parts that last and parts that change fast.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.720
Your balance organs report both, on two kinds of nerve fibre.

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.760
Regular afferents fire like a steady clock.

4
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.980
They carry sustained motion in their firing rate.

5
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.880
Irregular afferents fire unevenly.

6
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.860
They carry rapid changes in the precise timing of their spikes.

7
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.200
Two channels, one head turn.

8
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.900
Together they feed the reflexes that keep your gaze and posture steady.

9
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.630
Where does each channel come from?

10
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.910
Recordings from single fibres, traced back with dye, built a map.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:44.570
Regular fibres mostly start in the outer zone, on type II hair cells with small

12
00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.630
bouton endings. Irregular fibres mostly start in the central zone, where

13
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:54.150
flask-shaped type I cells sit inside a cup-shaped ending called a calyx.

14
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.930
The map holds for populations, not for every fibre.

15
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:00.683
The calyx is built for speed.

16
00:01:01.643 --> 00:01:04.283
Both hair cell types release glutamate from vesicles.

17
00:01:05.123 --> 00:01:10.323
Yet a mature mouse type I cell still made its calyx fire when either of two proteins

18
00:01:10.323 --> 00:01:12.263
needed for vesicle release was missing.

19
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:16.723
Recordings in turtles, a different animal, point to a possible route.

20
00:01:17.863 --> 00:01:22.303
Current and potassium cross the narrow cleft directly, with no vesicles and no gap

21
00:01:22.303 --> 00:01:26.963
junctions. This non-quantal transmission has little synaptic delay.

22
00:01:28.017 --> 00:01:32.237
Reviews propose that this short delay serves the fast reflexes.

23
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:34.437
The channels form as the ear matures.

24
00:01:35.377 --> 00:01:40.157
In young mice, new potassium channels speed up hair cell signals, make type I

25
00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:43.937
transmission non-quantal, and make afferent firing more irregular.

26
00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:50.357
Clinicians use the fast channel too: sound and vibration tests of balance mostly

27
00:01:50.357 --> 00:01:51.857
drive the irregular fibres.

28
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:56.797
Afferents fire whether you turn your head or something turns it for you.

29
00:01:57.557 --> 00:02:02.637
In alert monkeys, Roy and Cullen found vestibular-only neurons that responded much

30
00:02:02.637 --> 00:02:04.197
less to the monkey's own turns.

31
00:02:05.037 --> 00:02:09.077
That cancelling held only when neck feedback matched what the motor command

32
00:02:09.077 --> 00:02:12.617
predicted. When the match failed, the response came back.

33
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.190
Keep three ideas in order.

34
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:19.650
The ear splits head motion into a sustained channel and a fast channel.

35
00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.710
The calyx can drive its fibre without vesicles, which is thought to keep the fast

36
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:30.090
channel fast. And in the vestibular-only neurons tested, the cancelling is central,

37
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.210
and it held only when neck feedback matched the prediction.

38
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.350
You still feel your own turns; what changes is the reflex that would fight them.
